  "member_id": "olsson-eva",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B045",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and experimental realization of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B011",
    "B001"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. B045 remains the most clearly bounded invention on the shortlist: the magic-wavelength lattice-clock principle made it possible to interrogate confined atomic ensembles without the otherwise limiting trapping shift, and the defining experimental work established a reproducible new precision-measurement capability. Katori and Ye remain the most defensible compact allocation because the proposed citation joins the originating physical principle to its decisive experimental realization, while avoiding credit for optical clocks generally or for subsequent performance records. Its extensive experimental maturity is important evidence that this was a genuine new instrument for physics, not an attractive but untested proposal.",
    "case_against": "The remaining concern is historical precision. We must be certain that Katori and Ye, rather than a wider group of early contributors, exhaust the indispensable original conceptual and experimental roles for the narrowly stated lattice-clock achievement. The case also fails if it is framed merely as improved timekeeping; its merit must rest on the distinct magic-wavelength architecture and the physical access it created.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that Katori's magic-wavelength concept and Ye's foundational experimental advances give B045 unusually clean two-person attribution, while requiring separation from the wider optical-clock history.",
        "response": "I agree. This is the decisive virtue of B045, provided the historical review verifies the stated division of work. The final wording should retain both invention and experimental realization, which recognizes the physical principle and its convincing establishment without turning later metrological refinements into the prize."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson asks whether B045 is a fundamental discovery or instead an exceptional metrological implementation, and prefers B001 as a controllable many-body laboratory.",
        "response": "The question correctly sharpens the standard. I find B045 prize-worthy because the magic-wavelength architecture is a discrete invention that altered the attainable measurement regime, not because clocks achieved an impressive numerical record. B001 is a profound alternative, but its unresolved choice between Cirac and Greiner demonstrates a material difficulty in assigning exact credit under its narrow optical-lattice citation."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson favors B011 for its quantum-spin-Hall prediction and landmark realization, but notes the need to delimit it from the broader topological-matter history.",
        "response": "I continue to regard B011 as a strong alternative with a compelling theory-to-experiment arc. Yet its requested boundary and attribution review remain more consequential than for B045. The lattice-clock achievement can be stated as one specific physical invention and defining realization, with a clearer two-person allocation."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival priority evidence confirms that Katori and Ye are the indispensable originator and defining experimental developer for the narrowly framed optical-lattice-clock achievement.",
      "Whether the final citation can make clear that the award is for the magic-wavelength lattice-clock architecture rather than all optical clocks or later accuracy advances."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No conflict is apparent from the supplied profile; I have no identified collaboration, institutional connection, or other relevant relationship with the proposed laureates."
  }
}
